  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn employee earning purely compensation income from one employer is generally not required to file an annual Income Tax Return (ITR) if their taxable income does not exceed P250,000 and the income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individuals whose taxable income does not exceed P250,000 are not required to file an ITR, provided that their income tax has been withheld correctly.\n\n### Interpretation\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor employees earning solely from one employer, this provision simplifies tax compliance, as they may not need to file an ITR if their income is below the specified threshold and taxes have been withheld correctly. This reduces administrative burdens and allows employees to focus on their work without the added concern of annual tax filings, provided they meet the criteria outlined in the NIRC.",
